diff --git a/docs/producer-memory.md b/docs/producer-memory.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6a3c212
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/producer-memory.md
@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@
+# Explicit cached producer memory
+
+`reims_vgpu_vulkan::producer` is an opt-in Linux/Android interop API for one
+contiguous allocation shared, in separate epochs, by a native CPU consumer and
+the Metal-shaped Vulkan runtime. It does not replace ordinary guest RAM,
+`new_buffer_over_host_memory`, the guest remap table, or the shared-buffer path.
+
+## Allocation and identity
+
+`Producer::new(&runtime_device, bytes)` allocates on the supplied **logical
+runtime device**. It queries the actual runtime buffer usage, opaque-FD export
+support, dedicated-allocation requirements, memory-type bits, and cache atom
+size. A compatible `HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_CACHED` type is required. It does not
+retry with uncached memory or pretend that noncoherent memory is coherent.
+
+`Producer::system_default` and `rvg_producer_create` select and own a runtime
+device through reims' ordinary device selection. The C constructor is not an
+attachment to an existing `RvgDevice`. The reported `device_registry_id` names
+the physical adapter; equality does not prove that two objects share a logical
+Vulkan device. An embedding must use `Producer::new` with its actual device.
+
+One allocation has one runtime `Buffer`. Every binding uses that object's
+identity and an offset. The adopted descriptor is `Private`, with no runtime
+CPU contents pointer, so a cached noncoherent mapping never enters a path that
+promises Metal `Shared` coherence. The descriptor's retained backing owner
+keeps the raw Vulkan buffer, memory and device alive through runtime memory
+holds and commands. This requires metal2vulkan's adopted-buffer descriptor
+and retained-backing API.
+
+The CPU registry belongs to one process/address space. It records verified
+virtual spans, their offsets, a retained allocation owner, and non-reused
+registration generations. Resolution validates the complete resource before
+returning a physical span. Split or moved CPU pieces may resolve to the same
+contiguous allocation range. Holes, repeated pages, different owners and
+discontiguous offsets are refused. Descriptor numbers and reused virtual
+addresses never establish allocation identity. An outstanding resolution
+retains its allocation after a registration is removed.
+
+## Ownership and cache maintenance
+
+| State | Permitted work | Exit condition |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Idle | Begin one CPU or GPU epoch | Checked generation assigned |
+| CPU | Scalar access through the live mapping or qualified native provider | All accesses stopped and all aliases/exports retired |
+| Flushing / GPU | Whole-allocation flush, encode, commit and wait | Successful runtime completion and status |
+| Invalidating | Whole-allocation invalidate | Successful invalidate returns ownership to Idle |
+| Poisoned | Inspection only | No reuse; retained to process teardown |
+
+The runtime ends its submitted batches with a barrier to host access before
+completion signals. `submit` waits for runtime completion, checks its error and
+status, and then invalidates the complete mapping. Completion alone does not
+release the CPU gate. Full-size cache operations start at offset zero and cover
+the allocation's complete noncoherent cache atoms. Metadata, ownership locks
+and counters live in ordinary memory; the bridge issues no atomics against
+producer pages.
+
+The Rust `submit` callback is unsafe interop. It may encode only into the given
+command buffer, without retaining either supplied object, installing callbacks,
+submitting separately, signaling CPU-visible events, using another queue, or
+accessing a CPU alias. Thus it cannot describe a mid-command CPU handoff. The
+C upload/download calls implement only complete-buffer blits with a five-second
+completion wait. Allocation, translation and driver calls before that wait are
+not covered by its deadline; an external process watchdog is appropriate for
+diagnostics.
+
+Dropping an unfinished CPU epoch, abandoning a native export, failing a cache
+operation, failing a command, or timing out poisons the allocation. It never
+guesses that outstanding work stopped. The last backing release destroys Vulkan
+objects only from Idle; an uncertain state retains them and their device until
+process teardown. This intentional retention is preferable to reusing or freeing
+storage that a native worker or the GPU may still access.
+
+## Native mappings and retirement
+
+An opaque Vulkan FD is not a generic POSIX memory-file layout. The native
+consumer must use a provider qualified for its actual driver and allocation.
+The API promises neither nonzero-offset `mmap` nor arbitrary remapping. A
+qualified full offset-zero mapping can be registered with `register_alias`,
+which retains its mapping owner. Dropping the registration unregisters its
+generation, releases the mapping, then retires the ownership hold.
+
+`NativeView` and `RvgProducerNative` retain their producer independently of the
+public producer handle. The returned FD is borrowed and CLOEXEC. It may be
+duplicated or transferred, but closing an FD does not revoke existing mappings.
+Retirement is an unsafe assertion that every recipient, descendant, transferred
+copy and mapping is permanently quiescent. A supervisor can establish this by
+reaping its complete owned process tree. Only then can the CPU epoch end and
+the GPU begin. Replayed ends and retirements are refused.
+
+The canonical CPU pointer is valid only for its epoch and logical byte length.
+All accesses pause during an attempted end; they may resume after a refusal
+only while that same CPU epoch still owns the allocation. After a successful
+end, no pointer from that epoch may be used again.
+
+## Diagnostic and integration boundary
+
+The `producer_memory` example takes an assembled copy of the repository-authored
+metal2vulkan `runtime_examples.ll` fixture. Its canonical-only mode exercises
+upload, compute, independent readback, CPU visibility, export lifetime and
+poisoning without assuming an opaque-FD POSIX layout:
+
+```sh
+cargo run -p reims-vgpu-vulkan --example producer_memory -- \
+    /path/to/runtime_examples.air --canonical-only
+```
+
+Without `--canonical-only`, it additionally requires and checks a complete
+POSIX mapping before registering selected aliases, then checks alias writes
+against the canonical mapping. Failure is not a native-alias qualification.
+The example's 32 KiB geometry and five-second waits are diagnostic choices.
+
+General guest integration still needs shared device ownership, producer-backed
+QEMU region allocation, native mapping updates, guest gather/remap registration,
+resource lifetime hooks, and CPU/GPU handoffs at every guest-visible event.
+Fragmented resources need a separately designed representation; this API refuses
+them. Concurrent vCPUs, producer atomics, instruction fetch, suspend/resume,
+ordinary-app execution and an iOS boot are outside this API's qualification.
